arts that do not depend on each other (separate cases or separate quantities), solve those parts in a parallel block.\n\nWithin each parallel block:\nBegin the block with <Parallel>, then list the parts inside <Goal> and </Goal>, one per <Outline> and </Outline>, numbered 1:, 2:, ...\nWrite one path per outline with the same number, enclosed in <Path> and </Path>. A path must not use results of other paths, as the paths are generated simultaneously and independently.\nAfter the last path, combine the results of the paths in <Conclusion> and </Conclusion>, close the block with </Parallel> and continue the solution.\n\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: Paulo wants to get a burger meal that costs $6. Aside from that, he also wants to get a soda which costs 1/3 as much as the burger meal. While on the counter, Jeremy asked Paulo to get him 2 of each item Paulo is going to get. How much will they be charged for their orders combined?', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: In the human body the digestion of proteins takes place primarily in which two organs?\n\nA. Mouth and stomach\nB. Stomach and small intestine\nC. Liver and gall bladder\nD. Pancreas and large intestine\n\nThe final result is the letter of the correct option.', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nWhen the work splits into parts that do not depend on each other (separate cases or separate quantities), solve those parts in a parallel block.\n\nWithin each parallel block:\nBegin the block with <Parallel>, then list the parts inside <Goal> and </Goal>, one per <Outline> and </Outline>, numbered 1:, 2:, ...\nWrite one path per outline with the same number, enclosed in <Path> and </Path>. A path must not use results of other paths, as the paths are generated simultaneously and independently.\nAfter the last path, combine the results of the paths in <Conclusion> and </Conclusion>, close the block with </Parallel> and continue the solution.\n\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: Two cards are chosen at random from a standard 52-card deck.  What is the probability that both cards are numbers (2 through 10) totaling to 12?', "Solve the following problem step by step.\nWhen the work splits into parts that do not depend on each other (separate cases or separate quantities), solve those parts in a parallel block.\n\nWithin each parallel block:\nBegin the block with <Parallel>, then list the parts inside <Goal> and </Goal>, one per <Outline> and </Outline>, numbered 1:, 2:, ...\nWrite one path per outline with the same number, enclosed in <Path> and </Path>. A path must not use results of other paths, as the paths are generated simultaneously and independently.\nAfter the last path, combine the results of the paths in <Conclusion> and </Conclusion>, close the block with </Parallel> and continue the solution.\n\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: On the refrigerator, MATHEMATICS is spelled out with $11$ magnets, one letter per magnet. Two vowels and four consonants fall off and are put away in a bag. If the T's, M's, and A's are indistinguishable, how many distinct possible collections of letters could be put in the bag?", 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nWhen the work splits into parts that do not depend on each other (separate cases or separate quantities), solve those parts in a parallel block.\n\nWithin each parallel block:\nBegin the block with <Parallel>, then list the parts inside <Goal> and </Goal>, one per <Outline> and </Outline>, numbered 1:, 2:, ...\nWrite one path per outline with the same number, enclosed in <Path> and </Path>. A path must not use results of other paths, as the paths are generated simultaneously and independently.\nAfter the last path, combine the results of the paths in <Conclusion> and </Conclusion>, close the block with </Parallel> and continue the solution.\n\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: Simplify $$\\sqrt{6+4\\sqrt2}+\\sqrt{6-4\\sqrt2}.$$', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: A school has 15 classrooms. One-third of these classrooms have 30 desks in each classroom and the rest have 25 desks in each classroom. Only one student can sit at one desk. How many students can this school accommodate so that everyone has their own desk?', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: Which characteristic is found ONLY in mammals?\n\nA. eyes that detect color\nB. glands that make milk\nC. skin that absorbs oxygen\nD. bodies that are protected by scales\n\nThe final result is the letter of the correct option.', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: One way that students can help conserve natural resources at school is by\n\nA. recycling aluminum cans.\nB. emptying waste baskets.\nC. wearing warmer clothes.\nD. eating all of their lunch.\n\nThe final result is the letter of the correct option.', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: What is the sum of all integer values of $n$ such that $\\frac{20}{2n - 1}$ is an integer?', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: Water is a very important part of the physical weathering of rock. Which of these properties of water is most important in causing some of the physical weathering of rock?\n\nA. Water is a liquid at room temperature.\nB. Water can contain different minerals.\nC. Water expands when it freezes.\nD. Water dissolves many chemicals.\n\nThe final result is the letter of the correct option.', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nWhen the work splits into parts that do not depend on each other (separate cases or separate quantities), solve those parts in a parallel block.\n\nWithin each parallel block:\nBegin the block with <Parallel>, then list the parts inside <Goal> and </Goal>, one per <Outline> and </Outline>, numbered 1:, 2:, ...\nWrite one path per outline with the same number, enclosed in <Path> and </Path>. A path must not use results of other paths, as the paths are generated simultaneously and independently.\nAfter the last path, combine the results of the paths in <Conclusion> and </Conclusion>, close the block with </Parallel> and continue the solution.\n\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: Let $f(x) = x^2 + 6x + c$ for all real numbers $x$, where $c$ is some real number.  For what values of $c$ does $f(f(x))$ have exactly $3$ distinct real roots?', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: What is the greatest common factor of 30, 90 and 75?', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nWhen the work splits into parts that do not depend on each other (separate cases or separate quantities), solve those parts in a parallel block.\n\nWithin each parallel block:\nBegin the block with <Parallel>, then list the parts inside <Goal> and </Goal>, one per <Outline> and </Outline>, numbered 1:, 2:, ...\nWrite one path per outline with the same number, enclosed in <Path> and </Path>. A path must not use results of other paths, as the paths are generated simultaneously and independently.\nAfter the last path, combine the results of the paths in <Conclusion> and </Conclusion>, close the block with </Parallel> and continue the solution.\n\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: Alex needs to catch a train. The train arrives randomly some time between 1:00 and 2:00, waits for 10 minutes, and then leaves. If Alex also arrives randomly between 1:00 and 2:00, what is the probability that the train will be there when Alex arrives?', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: Given that $2x + y = 4$ and $x + 2y = 5$, find $5x^2 + 8xy + 5y^2$.', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: Oshea bought 200 basil seeds. He wants to plant them in planters. He has 4 large planters that can hold 20 seeds each. He also has small planters that can hold 4 seeds each. How many small planters will he need to plant all the basil seeds?', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: Condensation in the water cycle can best be described as the result of water vapor\n\nA. rising into the atmosphere, cooling and changing into a liquid form.\nB. rising into the atmosphere, warming and changing into a gaseous form.\nC. changing from a liquid form into a solid and forming snow.\nD. changing from a gas form into a solid and forming hail.\n\nThe final result is the letter of the correct option.', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nWhen the work splits into parts that do not depend on each other (separate cases or separate quantities), solve those parts in a parallel block.\n\nWithin each parallel block:\nBegin the block with <Parallel>, then list the parts inside <Goal> and </Goal>, one per <Outline> and </Outline>, numbered 1:, 2:, ...\nWrite one path per outline with the same number, enclosed in <Path> and </Path>. A path must not use results of other paths, as the paths are generated simultaneously and independently.\nAfter the last path, combine the results of the paths in <Conclusion> and </Conclusion>, close the block with </Parallel> and continue the solution.\n\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: Simplify $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{4}}{\\sqrt{7}}$ and rationalize the denominator of the resulting fraction.', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nWhen the work splits into parts that do not depend on each other (separate cases or separate quantities), solve those parts in a parallel block.\n\nWithin each parallel block:\nBegin the block with <Parallel>, then list the parts inside <Goal> and </Goal>, one per <Outline> and </Outline>, numbered 1:, 2:, ...\nWrite one path per outline with the same number, enclosed in <Path> and </Path>. A path must not use results of other paths, as the paths are generated simultaneously and independently.\nAfter the last path, combine the results of the paths in <Conclusion> and </Conclusion>, close the block with </Parallel> and continue the solution.\n\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: The numbers from 1 to 150, inclusive, are placed in a bag and a number is randomly selected from the bag. What is the probability it is not a perfect power (integers that can be expressed as $x^{y}$ where $x$ is an integer and $y$ is an integer greater than 1. For example, $2^{4}=16$ is a perfect power, while $2\\times3=6$ is not a perfect power)? Express your answer as a common fraction.', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nWhen the work splits into parts that do not depend on each other (separate cases or separate quantities), solve those parts in a parallel block.\n\nWithin each parallel block:\nBegin the block with <Parallel>, then list the parts inside <Goal> and </Goal>, one per <Outline> and </Outline>, numbered 1:, 2:, ...\nWrite one path per outline with the same number, enclosed in <Path> and </Path>. A path must not use results of other paths, as the paths are generated simultaneously and independently.\nAfter the last path, combine the results of the paths in <Conclusion> and </Conclusion>, close the block with </Parallel> and continue the solution.\n\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: Evaluate \\[\\frac 2{\\log_4{2000^6}} + \\frac 3{\\log_5{2000^6}},\\]giving your answer as a fraction in lowest terms.', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: What is the value of $f(-1)$ if $f(x)=x^{2}-2x$?', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: A teacher asks her class what the value of $\\left(5^2-4^2\\right)^3$ is.  What is the value of the expression?', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: John decides to replace all his hats.  He has enough hats to wear a different one every day for 2 weeks.  If each hat costs $50 how much do his hats cost?', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: Find $a$ such that $ax^2+12x+9$ is the square of a binomial.', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: Express the following as a common fraction: $\\sqrt[3]{4\\div 13.5}$.', "Solve the following problem step by step.\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: Juanico is 4 years less than half the age of Gladys. If Gladys will be 40 years old ten years from now, calculate Juanico's age 30 years from now.", 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: The value of $\\sqrt{73}$ is between two positive, consecutive integers. What is the product of these two integers?', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: Find the sum of the values of $x$ which satisfy $x^2 +1992x = 1993$.', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: The roots of the equation $2x^2-mx+n=0$ sum to 6 and multiply to 10. What is the value of $m+n$?', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: If $(x+2)(x-3)=14$, find the product of the possible values of $x$.', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nWhen the work splits into parts that do not depend on each other (separate cases or separate quantities), solve those parts in a parallel block.\n\nWithin each parallel block:\nBegin the block with <Parallel>, then list the parts inside <Goal> and </Goal>, one per <Outline> and </Outline>, numbered 1:, 2:, ...\nWrite one path per outline with the same number, enclosed in <Path> and </Path>. A path must not use results of other paths, as the paths are generated simultaneously and independently.\nAfter the last path, combine the results of the paths in <Conclusion> and </Conclusion>, close the block with </Parallel> and continue the solution.\n\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: Sue owns 11 pairs of shoes: six identical black pairs, three identical brown pairs and two identical gray pairs. If she picks two shoes at random, what is the probability that they are the same color and that one is a left shoe and the other is a right shoe? Express your answer as a common fraction.', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nWhen the work splits into parts that do not depend on each other (separate cases or separate quantities), solve those parts in a parallel block.\n\nWithin each parallel block:\nBegin the block with <Parallel>, then list the parts inside <Goal> and </Goal>, one per <Outline> and </Outline>, numbered 1:, 2:, ...\nWrite one path per outline with the same number, enclosed in <Path> and </Path>. A path must not use results of other paths, as the paths are generated simultaneously and independently.\nAfter the last path, combine the results of the paths in <Conclusion> and </Conclusion>, close the block with </Parallel> and continue the solution.\n\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: On an old-fashioned bicycle the front wheel has a radius of $2.5$ feet and the back wheel has a radius of $4$ inches.  If there is no slippage, how many revolutions will the back wheel make while the front wheel makes $100$ revolutions?', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: The three roots of the equation \\[ax^3+bx^2+cx+d=0\\]are $1,$ $2,$ and $3.$ Compute $\\frac{c}{d}.$', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: Let $f(x)$ be a function defined for all positive real numbers satisfying the conditions $f(x) > 0$ for all $x > 0$ and\n\\[f(x - y) = \\sqrt{f(xy) + 2}\\]for all $x > y > 0.$  Determine $f(2009).$', 'Solve the following problem step by step.\nEnd your response with a line starting with Final Answer: followed by the final result.\n\nProblem: When rolling a fair 6-sided die, what is the probabili